⟦400⟧{{smaller block|89. ''Examples of Applications.''—The following are some examples of cases in which the above methods may be applied to the calculation of areas and integrals.}}

⟦500⟧{{smaller block|(i) ''Construction of Mathematical Tables.''—Even where <math>u</math> is an explicit function of <math>x</math>, so that <math>\int^x udx</math> may be expressed in terms of <math>x</math>, it is often more convenient, for construction of a table of values of such an integral, to use finite-difference formulae. The formula of § 76 may (see {{sc|Differences, Calculus of}}) be written
{{block center|<math>\int^x udx = h.\mu\sigma u + h\left(-\tfrac{1}{12}\mu\delta u + \tfrac{11}{720}\mu\delta^3 u - \dots\right)</math>}}
{{block center|<math>= \mu\sigma\left(hu - \tfrac{1}{12}\delta^2 hu + \tfrac{11}{720}\delta^4 hu - \dots\right),</math>}}
{{block center|<math>\int^x udx = h.\sigma u + h\left(\tfrac{1}{24}\delta u - \tfrac{17}{5760}\delta^3 u + \dots\right)</math>}}
{{block center|<math>= \sigma\left(hu + \tfrac{1}{24}\delta^2 hu - \tfrac{17}{5760}\delta^4 hu + \dots\right).</math>}}
The second of these is usually the more convenient. Thus, to construct a table of values of <math>\int^x udx</math> by intervals of <math>h</math> in <math>x</math>, we first form a table of values of <math>hu</math> for the intermediate values of <math>x</math>, from this obtain a table of values of <math>\left(1 + \tfrac{1}{24}\delta^2 - \tfrac{17}{5760}\delta^4 + \dots\right) hu</math> for these values of <math>x</math>, and then construct the table of <math>\int^x udx</math> by successive additions. Attention must be given to the possible accumulation of errors due to the small errors in the values of <math>u</math>. Each of the above formulae involves an arbitrary constant; but this disappears when we start the additions from a known value of <math>\int^x udx</math>.}}

⟦1200⟧{{smaller block|The process may be repeated. Thus we have
{{block center|<math>\int^x\int^x udx\,dx = \left(\sigma + \tfrac{1}{24}\delta - \tfrac{17}{5760}\delta^3 + \dots\right)^2 h^2 u</math>}}
{{block center|<math>= \left(\sigma^2 + \tfrac{1}{12} - \tfrac{1}{240}\delta^2 + \tfrac{31}{60480}\delta^4 - \tfrac{289}{3628800}\delta^6 + \dots\right) h^2 u</math>}}
{{block center|<math>= \sigma^2\left(h^2 u + \tfrac{1}{12}\delta^2 h^2 u - \tfrac{1}{240}\delta^4 h^2 u + \dots\right).</math>}}
Here there are two arbitrary constants, which may be adjusted in various ways.}}

⟦1500⟧{{smaller block|The formulae may be used for extending the accuracy of tables, in cases where, if <math>v</math> represents the quantity tabulated, <math>hdv/dx</math> or <math>h^2d^2v/dx^2</math> can be conveniently expressed in terms of <math>v</math> and <math>x</math> to a greater degree of accuracy than it could be found from the table. The process practically consists in using the table as it stands for improving the first or second differences of <math>v</math> and then building up the table afresh.}}

⟦1700⟧{{smaller block|(ii) ''Life Insurance.''—The use of quadrature-formulae is important in actuarial work, where the fundamental tables are based on experience, and the formulae applying these tables involve the use of the tabulated values and their differences.}}

⟦1800⟧{{smaller block|90. The following are instances of the application of approximative formulae to the calculation of the volumes of solids.}}

⟦1900⟧{{smaller block|(i) ''Timber Measure.''—To find the quantity of timber in a trunk with parallel ends, the areas of a few sections must be calculated as accurately as possible, and a formula applied. As the measurements can only be rough, the trapezoidal rule is the most appropriate in ordinary cases.}}

⟦2000⟧{{smaller block|(ii) ''Gauging.''—To measure the volume of a cask, it may be assumed that the interior is approximately a portion of a spheroidal figure. The formula applied can then be either Simpson's rule or a rule based on Gauss's theorem for two ordinates (§ 56). In the latter case the two sections are taken at distances <math>\pm\tfrac{1}{2}\text{H}/\sqrt{3} = \pm\cdot2887\text{H}</math> from the middle section, where H is the total internal length; and their arithmetic mean is taken to be the mean section of the cask. Allowance must of course be made for the thickness of the wood.}}

⟦2300⟧{{smaller block|91. Certain approximate formulae for the length of an arc of a circle are obtained by methods similar to those of §§ 71 and 79. Let <math>a</math> be the radius of a circle, and <math>\theta</math> (circular measure) the unknown angle subtended by an arc. Then, if we divide <math>\theta</math> into <math>m</math> equal parts, and <math>\text{L}_1</math> denotes the sum of the corresponding chords, so that <math>\text{L}_1 = 2ma \sin\,(\theta/2m)</math>, the true length of the arc is <math>\text{L}_1 + a\theta\left\{\frac{\phi^2}{3!} - \frac{\phi^4}{5!} + \dots\right\}</math>, where <math>\phi = \theta/2m</math>. Similarly, if <math>\text{L}_2</math> represents the sum of the chords when <math>m</math> (assumed even) is replaced by <math>\tfrac{1}{2}m</math>, we have an expression involving <math>\text{L}_2</math> and <math>2\phi</math>. The method of § 71 then shows that, by taking <math>\tfrac{1}{3}(4\text{L}_1 - \text{L}_2)</math> as the value of the arc, we get rid of terms in <math>\phi^2</math>. If we use <math>c_1</math> to represent the chord of the whole arc, <math>c_2</math> the chord of half the arc, and <math>c_4</math> the chord of one quarter of the arc, then corresponding to (i) and (iii) of § 70 or § 79 we have <math>\tfrac{1}{3}(8c_2 - c_1)</math> and <math>\tfrac{1}{45}(256c_4 - 40c_2 + c_1)</math> as approximations to the length of the arc. The first of these is ''Huygens's rule.''}}

⟦3000⟧{{smaller block|{{sc|References.}}—For applications of the prismoidal formula, see Alfred Lodge, ''Mensuration for Senior Students'' (1895). Other works on elementary mensuration are G. T. Chivers, ''Elementary Mensuration'' (1904); R. W. K. Edwards, ''Elementary Plane and Solid Mensuration'' (1902); William H. Jackson, ''Elementary Solid Geometry'' (1907); P. A. Lambert, ''Computation and Mensuration'' (1907). A. E. Pierpoint's ''Mensuration Formulae'' (1902) is a handy collection. Rules for calculation of areas are also given in such works as F. Castle, ''Manual of Practical Mathematics'' (1903); F. C. Clarke, ''Practical Mathematics'' (1907); C. T. Millis, ''Technical Arithmetic and Geometry''}}
